Scott's Example 1.3 takes tokens \(\Delta = \{0, 1, 2\}\) and neighbourhoods \(\mathcal{D} = \{\{0, 1, 2\}, \{1, 2\}, \{2\}\}\), drawn as three nested boxes, and says that the passage to the total element represented by \(2\) splits into two steps, that \(0\) and \(1\) do not represent total elements, and that the direction of approximation is unique. `Token` is that three-element set, `master` is \(\{0, 1, 2\}\), `twelve` is \(\{1, 2\}\), and `two` is \(\{2\}\); `mem_iff` says a set of tokens is a neighbourhood exactly when it equals one of those three, and `neighborhoodSystem` gives the system whose intersection law is `inter_eq`, the nine-case check that any two of the three meet in the master, in \(\{1, 2\}\), or in \(\{2\}\). `bot` is the filter whose only neighbourhood is the master, `elemTwelve` is the filter of the master and \(\{1, 2\}\), and `elemTwo` is the filter of all three, so the total element is the principal filter of \(\{2\}\). `bot_lt_elemTwelve` is the first strict step: every neighbourhood of the bottom element is the master, which belongs to the intermediate element, and the reverse inequality would put \(\{1, 2\}\) into the bottom filter, forcing it to equal the master, which `master_not_subset_twelve` forbids because \(0\) lies in the master. `elemTwelve_lt_elemTwo` is the second strict step: the master and \(\{1, 2\}\) belong to the total filter, and the reverse inequality would put \(\{2\}\) into the intermediate filter, forcing \(\{2\}\) to be the master or to equal \(\{1, 2\}\), which `twelve_not_subset_two` forbids because \(1\) lies in \(\{1, 2\}\). `element_classification` says every element is one of these three filters, by cases on membership of \(\{2\}\) and of \(\{1, 2\}\), and `elemTwo_maximal` says any element above the total filter equals it, so the approximation order is that single chain and the filter of \(\{2\}\) is the unique maximal element. **Theorem:** yes — the formal system is those three neighbourhoods, and its elements are exactly the two-step chain from the master filter through the filter of \(\{1, 2\}\) to the filter of \(\{2\}\). **Proof:** yes — the system law is the nine-case intersection analysis, and each strict step is the failure of the reverse inequality on a token that separates the neighbourhoods.
